# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
hume-enquiry-1.md:1: argue/readings: S3 is read 2 ways: hume-enquiry-1.logic.r2 has nothing from it; hume-enquiry-1.logic.r2.2 and hume-enquiry-1.logic.r2.3 have "types_make_present"; they differ when nothing is true, which the first allows
hume-enquiry-1.md:1: argue/answered: S3: answered yes, which keeps hume-enquiry-1.logic.r2, to: Suppose "sensible types and images render the objects of faith more present than intellectual contemplation alone can" is false. Is S3, "We shadow out the objects of our faith, say they, in sensible types and images, and render them more present to us by the immediate presence of these types, than it is possible for us to do merely by an intellectual view and contemplation.", true then?
hume-enquiry-1.md:1: argue/readings: S5 is read 2 ways: hume-enquiry-1.logic.r2 and hume-enquiry-1.logic.r2.2 have "concur"; hume-enquiry-1.logic.r2.3 has "concur" and "resemblance_common ∧ concur → principle_proved"; they differ when only "the effect of resemblance in enlivening the ideas is very common" and "in every case a resemblance and a present impression concur" are true, which the first allows
hume-enquiry-1.md:1: argue/answered: S5: answered no, which keeps hume-enquiry-1.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "the effect of resemblance in enlivening the ideas is very common" and "in every case a resemblance and a present impression concur" are true and "we are abundantly supplied with experiments to prove the reality of the foregoing principle" is false. Is S5, "I shall only infer from these practices, and this reasoning, that the effect of resemblance in enlivening the ideas is very common; and as in every case a resemblance and a present impression must concur, we are abundantly supplied with experiments to prove the reality of the foregoing principle.", true then?
hume-enquiry-1.logic.r2: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 3 ways: hume-enquiry-1.logic.r2 has "practices_enliven ∧ sensible_greater ∧ convey_to_resembling → resemblance_common" and "resemblance_common ∧ concur → principle_proved"; hume-enquiry-1.logic.r2.2 has nothing from it; they differ when only "external motions, postures and actions enliven the devotees' devotion and quicken their fervour, which would otherwise decay", "sensible objects have always a greater influence on the fancy than any other", "sensible objects readily convey this influence to the ideas to which they are related and which they resemble", "the effect of resemblance in enlivening the ideas is very common" and "in every case a resemblance and a present impression concur" are true, which the second allows; hume-enquiry-1.logic.r2.3 has "practices_enliven ∧ types_make_present ∧ sensible_greater ∧ convey_to_resembling → resemblance_common"
hume-enquiry-1.logic.r2: argue/readings: U1: hume-enquiry-1.logic.r2 and hume-enquiry-1.logic.r2.3 take for granted "if (external motions, postures and actions enliven the devotees' devotion and quicken their fervour, which would otherwise decay and sensible objects have always a greater influence on the fancy than any other and sensible objects readily convey this influence to the ideas to which they are related and which they resemble), then the effect of resemblance in enlivening the ideas is very common"; hume-enquiry-1.logic.r2.2 does not
hume-enquiry-1.logic.r2: argue/answered: U1: answered yes, which keeps hume-enquiry-1.logic.r2 and hume-enquiry-1.logic.r2.3, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if (external motions, postures and actions enliven the devotees' devotion and quicken their fervour, which would otherwise decay and sensible objects have always a greater influence on the fancy than any other and sensible objects readily convey this influence to the ideas to which they are related and which they resemble), then the effect of resemblance in enlivening the ideas is very common"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
hume-enquiry-1.logic.r2: argue/readings: U2: hume-enquiry-1.logic.r2 and hume-enquiry-1.logic.r2.3 take for granted "if (the effect of resemblance in enlivening the ideas is very common and in every case a resemblance and a present impression concur), then we are abundantly supplied with experiments to prove the reality of the foregoing principle"; hume-enquiry-1.logic.r2.2 does not
hume-enquiry-1.logic.r2: argue/answered: U2: answered yes, which keeps hume-enquiry-1.logic.r2 and hume-enquiry-1.logic.r2.3, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if (the effect of resemblance in enlivening the ideas is very common and in every case a resemblance and a present impression concur), then we are abundantly supplied with experiments to prove the reality of the foregoing principle"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
hume-enquiry-1.logic.r2: argue/readings: U3: hume-enquiry-1.logic.r2 and hume-enquiry-1.logic.r2.3 take for granted "if (external motions, postures and actions enliven the devotees' devotion and quicken their fervour, which would otherwise decay and sensible types and images render the objects of faith more present than intellectual contemplation alone can and sensible objects have always a greater influence on the fancy than any other and sensible objects readily convey this influence to the ideas to which they are related and which they resemble), then the effect of resemblance in enlivening the ideas is very common"; hume-enquiry-1.logic.r2.2 does not
hume-enquiry-1.logic.r2: argue/answered: U3: answered yes, which keeps hume-enquiry-1.logic.r2 and hume-enquiry-1.logic.r2.3, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if (external motions, postures and actions enliven the devotees' devotion and quicken their fervour, which would otherwise decay and sensible types and images render the objects of faith more present than intellectual contemplation alone can and sensible objects have always a greater influence on the fancy than any other and sensible objects readily convey this influence to the ideas to which they are related and which they resemble), then the effect of resemblance in enlivening the ideas is very common"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
